                  "text": "**Analyzing Border Properties**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the rules for cell behavior based on border properties. Specifically, I'm noting the color assignments: top (4), bottom (8), left (1), and right (2). I have also noted that the corner value is 0. My next step is to understand how the \"gravitate\" function will apply these border colors to influence the inner cells, and will examine the way gravitate(source, destination) works.\n\n\n**Deconstructing the Gravitate Function**\n\nI'm now deep-diving into the `gravitate` function's mechanics. It seems `source` is a 1x1 cell and `destination` involves a `chain` with `fgpartition`. This involves filtering interior cells based on border colors. Specifically, after filling the interior and corners with -1 in x8, `fgpartition` extracts border edges as objects in x9, associating each with a color (4, 8, 1, 2 for top, bottom, left, right respectively). I'm examining how these border colors are then applied and filtered to influence interior cells via x16 and x19.\n\n\n**Clarifying Motion Dynamics**\n\nI'm zeroing in on the directional logic within `gravitate`. It hinges on `vmatching`, which I now see determines the axis of movement. When vertical match, movement is vertical. Otherwise, it is horizontal. Specifically, the top and bottom borders *always* vmatch cells on the interior with x-coordinates within the bounds of the top/bottom borders. The left border does *not* match, because it has j = 0. My conclusion is that if a source is located to the right of the left border, movement with it will be to the left, and if a source is located to the left of the right border, movement with it will be to the right.\n\n\n**Interpreting Movement Logic**\n\nI've clarified the directional movement within the system, focusing on how border conditions affect cell trajectories. I see now that leftward motion happens until adjacency with the left border, while rightward is similar. Also, an interior cell moves up/down until adjacency with the top/bottom border, respectively. Cells without border-matching colors are filtered out, clearing the interior back to background. I've also checked item 1 to confirm these rules apply.\n\n\n",
